[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-mate"]
[LessonTitle "The back-rank mate"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[TeacherNotes "The back rank is the row where the king starts | The king's own pawns can trap it | A rook or queen on an open file does the job"]
[Mistakes "Moving all the pawns in front of the king without making an escape square | Forgetting to check whether the file is open"]
[ClassQuestions "Which black pieces are blocking the king's escape? | Which squares does the rook attack on the eighth rank?"]

{The black king is stuck behind its own pawns. A rook check on the back row can be checkmate. [%csl Rg8,Rf7,Rg7,Rh7] [%cal Ge1e8]}
1. Re8# {The rook checks the king. The pawns block f7, g7 and h7. The rook also covers f8 and h8. No escape, so it is checkmate. [%cal Ge8f8,Ge8g8,Ge8h8]} 1-0

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-mate"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1"]
[Hint "Use your rook."]
[Hint "Go to the eighth row, on the d-file."]

{White to move. Find the checkmate.}
1. Rd8# {The same idea. The pawns trap their own king.} 1-0

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-mate"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1"]

{The pawns on f7, g7 and h7 take away the squares in front of the king, and the rook covers the squares beside it.}
1. Rd8# 1-0

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "make-luft"]
[LessonTitle "Defending: make a window"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 1"]
[TeacherNotes "Luft is a German word for air. It means an escape square for the king | Kids should check their own back rank every few moves | Any one of the f, g or h pawns can make the window"]
[Mistakes "Waiting until the rook is already on the file | Pushing a pawn that does not open a square next to the king"]
[ClassQuestions "Which squares could the king use after one pawn moves? | When should you make the window, now or later?"]

{It is Black's move. White threatens Re8 checkmate. Black needs an escape square. [%cal Re1e8] [%csl Re8]}
1... h6 {A small pawn move. Now the h7 square is empty. [%csl Gh7]} 2. Re8+ {Check, but this time the king has a way out.} 2... Kh7 {The king steps to h7. No checkmate!} *

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "make-luft"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 1"]
[Hint "Use a pawn."]
[Hint "Move the h-pawn one step, to h6."]

{Black to move. Push the h-pawn one square to make a window for the king.}
1... h6 {Now the king can use h7.} *

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "make-luft"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/5PPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 1"]

{A pawn move such as h6 gives the king a square. Kh8 still loses to Re8 checkmate.}
*

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-trade"]
[LessonTitle "Trade off the guard"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "r5k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/4RPPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[TeacherNotes "Two rooks together are stronger than one guard | Each trade keeps the attacker in charge | Count attackers and defenders of the e8 square"]
[Mistakes "Checking with only one rook when Black can take it for free | Forgetting that the black rook can take back"]
[ClassQuestions "How many white pieces attack e8? | How many black pieces guard it?"]

{Black has a rook guarding the back row. White has two rooks on the e-file. [%cal Ge1e8,Ge2e8,Ra8e8]}
1. Re8+ {White offers a trade. [%csl Re8]} 1... Rxe8 {The black rook has to capture or the king is lost.} 2. Rxe8# {The second rook takes back. It is checkmate again.} 1-0

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-trade"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "r5k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/4RPPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[Hint "Use a rook."]
[Hint "Check on e8 with the rook on e2."]

{White to move. Find the line. Start with the check on e8.}
1. Re8+ Rxe8 2. Rxe8# {Checkmate.} 1-0

[Event "Checkmate Patterns"]
[Lesson "back-rank-trade"]
[Step "line-drill"]
[FEN "r5k1/5ppp/8/8/8/8/4RPPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1"]
[Side "white"]
[Hint "Offer a trade on the e8 square."]

{Play the whole line as White.}
1. Re8+ Rxe8 2. Rxe8# *
